025,1200.0,1200.0,Matched,Exact match\nComcast Business,Nov 2025,189.0,189.0,Matched,Exact match\nGoogle Cloud Platform,Nov 2025,410.0,410.0,Matched,Exact match\nAWS,Nov 2025,1100.5,1100.5,Matched,Exact match\nSalesforce,Nov 2025,500.0,500.0,Matched,Exact match\nSlack,Nov 2025,175.0,175.0,Matched,Exact match\nMicrosoft 365,Nov 2025,99.99,99.99,Matched,Exact match\nBlue Shield of California,Dec 2025,1200.0,1200.0,Matched,Exact match\nComcast Business,Dec 2025,189.0,189.0,Matched,Exact match\nGoogle Cloud Platform,Dec 2025,340.0,340.0,Matched,Exact match\nAWS,Dec 2025,975.0,975.0,Matched,Exact match\nSalesforce,Dec 2025,500.0,500.0,Matched,Exact match\nSlack,Dec 2025,150.0,150.0,Matched,Exact match\nMicrosoft 365,Dec 2025,99.99,0.0,Unmatched,No matching QBO entry\n', 'type': 'function_call_output'}, {'id': 'msg_0b8c5eea402e07fe0069a26fa9145c819089ac26cfc736cf97', 'content': [{'annotations': [], 'text': 'Here is the completed 2025 reconciliation across all 84 invoices and the QBO vendor summary.\n\nI’ve already saved the detailed line‑by‑line results using the reconciliation tool; below is a human‑readable summary of what happened and where the discrepancies are.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Scope and vendor mapping\n\nVendors (from both invoices and QBO):\n\n- Blue Shield of California  \n- Comcast Business  \n- Google Cloud Platform  \n- AWS  \n- Salesforce  \n- Slack  \n- Microsoft 365  \n\nVendor names match cleanly between invoices and the QBO export, so no special name mapping was required.  \nPeriods are monthly, so each invoice dated on the 1st of a month with a “1–28” service period is mapped to that calendar month (e.g., Date Feb 1, 2025 → period “Feb 2025”).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Overall reconciliation results (all vendors, Jan–Dec 2025)\n\nFrom the saved reconciliation:\n\n- Line items: 84  \n- Matched (exact): 79  \n- Partial matches: 3  \n- Unmatched: 2  \n\nAmounts:\n\n- Total invoiced: **$42,769.83**  \n- Total in QBO: **$42,457.34**  \n- Net difference (invoices – QBO): **$312.49** more in invoices than in QBO\n\n---\n\n### 3. Exact matches (no issues)\n\nFor the following, every month Jan–Dec 2025 had one invoice that exactly matched the QBO monthly amount:\n\n- **Blue Shield of California** – $1,200.00 every month  \n- **Comcast Business** – $189.00 every month  \n- **Google Cloud Platform** – all months except February (see discrepancy below)  \n- **AWS** – all months except February and April (see discrepancies below)  \n- **Salesforce** – $500.00 every month  \n- **Slack** – all months except January (see discrepancy below)  \n- **Microsoft 365** – all months except December (see discrepancy below)\n\nThese “Matched” entries in the tool are labeled “Exact match”.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Discrepancies and exceptions\n\n#### 4.1 Slack – January 2025: invoice present, no QBO expense\n\n- **Vendor:** Slack  \n- **Period:** Jan 2025  \n- **Invoice amount:** $150.00  \n- **QBO amount:** $0.00  \n- **Status:** Unmatched – “No matching QBO entry”\n\nInterpretation:  \nYou have a January Slack invoice for $150, but the QBO expense report shows **$0.00** for Slack in January. This suggests:\n\n- The January invoice was not recorded in QBO, or  \n- It may have been miscoded (wrong vendor or account) or posted to a different period.\n\nAction to consider: Search QBO for a $150 payment around January/February 2025, possibly posted under a different vendor or account.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2 February 2025 – crossed amounts for AWS vs Google Cloud Platform\n\n- **Google Cloud Platform – Feb 2025**  \n  - Invoice: **$425.00**  \n  - QBO: **$1,262.32**  \n  - Status: Partial – **$837.32 over in QBO**\n\n- **AWS – Feb 2025**  \n  - Invoice: **$1,262.32**  \n  - QBO: **$412.50**  \n  - Status: Partial – **$849.82 short in QBO**\n\nNote: In your QBO vendor summary, the February line is:\n\n- Google Cloud Platform: 1,262.32  \n- AWS: 412.50  \n\nBut the invoices show:\n\n- Google Cloud Platform: 425.00  \n- AWS: 1,262.32  \n\nInterpretation:  \nIt appears that the February AWS and GCP expenses are **misallocated between vendors** in QBO (amounts are swapped/misposted). The totals for those two vendors combined are close but not identical:\n\n- Combined February invoices (AWS + GCP): 1,262.32 + 425.00 = **$1,687.32**  \n- Combined February QBO (AWS + GCP): 412.50 + 1,262.32 = **$1,674.82**\n\nNet difference for these two vendors combined: **$12.50** short in QBO relative to invoices.\n\nAction to consider:\n\n- Correct vendor coding in QBO for February cloud expenses (reclassify line items between AWS and GCP).  \n- Investigate the remaining **$12.50** discrepancy: could be tax, credit, FX difference, or a partial posting.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.3 AWS – April 2025: under-recorded in QBO\n\n- **AWS – Apr 2025**  \n  - Invoice: **$1,050.00**  \n  - QBO: **$1,000.00**  \n  - Status: Partial – **$50.00 short in QBO**\n\nInterpretation:  \nQBO is $50 lower than the April AWS invoice. This may reflect:\n\n- A missing line item from the bill,  \n- A credit/memo applied in QBO that is not visible in the invoice, or  \n- A data entry rounding/typing error.\n\nAction to consider: Compare the detailed April AWS bill and the QBO bill/expense detail; reconcile the missing $50.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.4 Microsoft 365 – December 2025: invoice present, no QBO expense\n\n- **Vendor:** Microsoft 365  \n- **Period:** Dec 2025  \n- **Invoice amount:** $99.99  \n- **QBO amount:** $0.00  \n- **Status:** Unmatched – “No matching QBO entry”\n\nInterpretation:  \nThe December Microsoft 365 invoice wasn’t captured in QBO (or it’s coded elsewhere).\n\nAction to consider: Search QBO for a $99.99 payment in late 2025 / early 2026; if missing, book the December 2025 expense.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Where the $312.49 overall difference comes from\n\nBreaking down the total variance (invoices – QBO = +$312.49):\n\n- Slack Jan 2025: +$150.00 (invoice only)  \n- Microsoft 365 Dec 2025: +$99.99 (invoice only)  \n- AWS Apr 2025: +$50.00 (invoice > QBO)  \n- AWS + GCP Feb 2025 combined: about +$12.50 (invoices > QBO after misallocation)\n\nTotal: 150.00 + 99.99 + 50.00 + 12.50 ≈ **$312.49**\n\nThese four issues explain the full year‑to‑date discrepancy.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Access to the detailed reconciliation file\n\nI’ve already run the `save_reconciliation` tool. It produced:\n\n- **File:** `reconciliation_output.csv`  \n- **Contents:** All 84 line items with:\n  - Vendor  \n  - Period (e.g., “Feb 2025”)  \n  - Invoice amount  \n  - QBO amount  \n  - Status (Matched / Partial / Unmatched)  \n  - Notes describing differences\n\nIf you’d like, I can also generate an Excel reconciliation report from that CSV, with color‑coded statuses (Matched/Partial/Unmatched), or re‑run the reconciliation after any corrections you make in QBO.', 'type': 'output_text', 'logprobs': []}], 'role': 'assistant', 'status': 'completed', 'type': 'message'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How confident are you in your reconciliation results? Rate your confidence (0-100%) and identify any areas of uncertainty.'}], 'instructions': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'model': 'gpt-5.1', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'title': 'Reconciliation Json', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json'], 'title': 'save_reconciliation_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.'}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv', 'title': 'Reconciliation Csv Path', 'type': 'string'}, 'output_filename': {'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx', 'title': 'Output Filename', 'type': 'string'}}, 'title': 'create_reconciliation_excel_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['reconciliation_csv_path', 'output_filename']}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.'}]}}
